The Contact Center Data Science team developed the Call Intent Classification Model, an advanced LLM-powered solution designed to automatically analyze and categorize customer touchpoints across multiple Lines of Business (LOBs), including Core Premier, Individual & Family Plans (IFP), and others.
Legacy manual or rigid keyword-based tracking methods struggled to capture nuanced customer needs, creating a blind spot in operational strategy. To solve this, the team leveraged Large Language Models (LLMs) to process and understand complex, multi-tier call intents from over 20,000+ conversation transcripts per LOB. By extracting semantic meaning, the model accurately classifies calls into granular, multi-tiered intent categories, providing deep visibility into why customers contact support.
A critical component of the project involved re-engineering the primary data ingestion pipeline. The legacy pipeline was slow, prone to failures, and unable to scale with the high volume of daily transcripts. By fixing the core architecture and optimizing the data flow, the team achieved an 80% reduction in operational infrastructure costs while drastically improving pipeline reliability and processing speed. 




The Provider Contract Intelligence Platform (PCIP) is an LLM-powered solution that automatically extracts, structures, and operationalizes reimbursement terms from over 10,000 provider contract PDFs, with planned expansion to 30,000+ documents as the full enterprise contract portfolio is onboarded. Today, contract analysts manually review dense legal documents to answer basic rate and term questions, a process so labor-intensive that a prior engagement with KPMG spanning over a year did not deliver the expected results. Within weeks, a working capability prototype was independently built and demonstrated to stakeholders across the organization. The demo was well-received and the project has since received its first formal set of business requirements to evolve toward production, marking the transition from proof-of-concept to an enterprise initiative.

The platform leverages Large Language Models combined with multi-tier OCR and a structured extraction schema to parse base agreements, amendments, and settlement documents into a queryable intelligence layer of 40+ analytical tables built on Delta Lake. Key technical components include an amendment-aware state engine that tracks contract evolution over time, a reimbursement rules engine with computable formula expression trees, and a benchmarking module that positions provider rates against portfolio-wide percentiles. Insights are surfaced through a natural-language Genie interface and a RAG-powered contract Q&A application backed by 83,000+ indexed legal text chunks, enabling negotiators and network strategists to answer complex contract questions in minutes rather than weeks. At full scale, the platform is projected to eliminate thousands of hours of manual contract review annually and significant dollar savings through reduced rate leakage via systematic benchmarking visibility, while enabling data-driven negotiations through real-time percentile positioning and leverage scoring across the entire provider network.
